On 05/08/2013 04:00 PM, kathleen...@psy.kuleuven.be wrote:
> Dear Lavaan-users,
>
> I recently started using lavaan, but encounter some convergence problems
> when trying to fit my models.
>
> I have a dataset with scores between 0 and 5 of 783 participants on 15
> variables. The scores are the sum of 5 very similar items (with
> correct=1, incorrect=0) on 15 different subtests. I tread the scores as
> ordered given the limited range of possible values and skewness of the
> data.

You could also try treating the variables as continuous, and use
estimator "MLR" (to handle skewness).

> lavaan WARNING: model has NOT converged!

At first sight, the problem seems to be that there is too much overlap
between the factors (too many shared items). I think you need at least 2
indicators per factor that are unique (ie zero loadings on all other
factors).

> lavaan WARNING: covariance matrix of latent variables is not positive definite

If you get this warning, try this:

eigen(inspect(fit, "cov.lv <http://cov.lv>"))$values

If the last value is very, very close to zero (say, -1e-015), you can
safely ignore this. This check is rather 'strict', and will be relaxed a
little bit in the next version.

> Also, a couple of months ago I performed a similar analysis with lavaan
> 0.5-11 and this worked fine, but since I updated to lavaan 0.5-12 I get
> a warning that the covariance matrix is not positive definite with
> exactly the same code.

This is just a new check added in 0.5-12. A bit too strict perhaps.

Yves.

